The '''2005 FIFA Club World Championship final''' was an [[association football]] match played between [[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]] of Brazil, the [[CONMEBOL]] club champions, and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] of England, the [[UEFA]] club champions, on 18 December 2005 at the [[International Stadium Yokohama]], Japan. It was the final match of the [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship]], a competition for the winners of the primary cup competitions of FIFA's continental members. The Club World Championship replaced the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]], which both teams had competed in before. São Paulo had won the Intercontinental Cup twice in [[1992 Intercontinental Cup|1992]] and [[1993 Intercontinental Cup|1993]], while Liverpool had lost twice in [[1981 Intercontinental Cup|1981]] and [[1984 Intercontinental Cup|1984]].

The teams qualified for the championship by winning their continent's primary cup competition. São Paulo won the [[2005 Copa Libertadores]], defeating Brazilian team [[Clube Atlético Paranaense|Atlético Paranaense]] [[2005 Copa Libertadores Finals|5–1]]. Liverpool won the [[2004–05 UEFA Champions League]], defeating Italian team [[A.C. Milan|Milan]] 3–2 in a [[Penalty shootout (association football)|penalty shootout]] after the match had finished [[2005 UEFA Champions League Final|3–3]]. Both teams played one match in the championship en route to the final. São Paulo beat [[2005 AFC Champions League]] winners [[Ittihad FC|Al-Ittihad]] 3–2, while Liverpool beat [[2005 CONCACAF Champions' Cup]] winners [[C.D. Saprissa|Deportivo Saprissa]] 3–0.

Watched by a crowd of 66,821, São Paulo took the lead in the first half when [[Mineiro (footballer, born 1975)|Mineiro]] scored. Liverpool missed a number of chances to equalise in the first half. In the second half they had the majority of the possession and also had three goals disallowed. Despite all their chances they were unable to score against São Paulo who held on to win the match 1–0.

==Route to the final==
[[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]] qualified for the [[FIFA Club World Cup|FIFA Club World Championship]] as the reigning [[Copa Libertadores]] winners. They had won the [[2005 Copa Libertadores]] beating [[Clube Atlético Paranaense|Atlético Paranaense]] [[2005 Copa Libertadores Finals|5–1]] over two-legs in the final.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/sports/soccer/tourneys/2005-07-15-copa-libertadores-final_x.htm |title=Sao Paulo rolls to third Copa Libertadores crown |work=USA Today |date=15 July 2005 |access-date=31 August 2011 |first=Tales |last=Azzoni }}</ref> It would be São Paulo's first appearance in the competition, although they had won the forerunner to the competition, the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]] twice, in [[1992 Intercontinental Cup|1992]] and [[1993 Intercontinental Cup|1993]].<ref name="rsssf">{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablest/toyota.html |title=Intercontinental Club Cup |publisher=Rec. Sport. Soccer Statistics Foundation (RSSSF) |date=30 April 2005 |access-date=31 August 2011 |first=Loris |first2=Karel |last=Magnani |last2=Stokkermans }}</ref>

[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] had qualified for the Club World Championship as a result of winning the [[2004–05 UEFA Champions League]]. They beat Italian team [[A.C. Milan|Milan]] 3–2 in a [[Penalty shootout (association football)|penalty shootout]] after the match had finished [[2005 UEFA Champions League Final|3–3]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2005/may/25/minutebyminute.championsleague |title=Liverpool 3-3 AC Milan |work=The Guardian |date=25 May 2005 |access-date=25 April 2011 |location=London |first=Barry |last=Glendenning }}</ref> Like São Paulo, Liverpool were competing in the competition for the first time, although they had appeared twice in the Intercontinental Cup, losing in [[1981 Intercontinental Cup|1981]] and [[1984 Intercontinental Cup|1984]].<ref name="rsssf" />

Both teams entered the competition in the semi-finals. São Paulo's opponents were the winners of the [[2005 AFC Champions League]], [[Ittihad FC|Al-Ittihad]]. A 3–2 victory with two goals from [[Márcio Amoroso|Amoroso]] and a penalty from goalkeeper [[Rogério Ceni]] ensured São Paulo's progression to the final.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2369759/Boys-from-Brazil-reach-Club-World-final.html |title=Boys from Brazil reach Club World final |work=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=15 December 2005 |access-date=29 August 2011 |first=Scott |last=Purchase }}</ref> Liverpool faced [[2005 CONCACAF Champions' Cup]] winners [[C.D. Saprissa|Deportivo Saprissa]]. They won the match 3–0, [[Peter Crouch]] scored twice and [[Steven Gerrard]] also scored as Liverpool reached the final.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/liverpool-3-deportivo-saprissa-0-crouch-double-as-liverpool-demolish-costa-ricans-to-reach-showpiece-final-519630.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/liverpool-3-deportivo-saprissa-0-crouch-double-as-liverpool-demolish-costa-ricans-to-reach-showpiece-final-519630.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |title=Liverpool 3 Deportivo Saprissa 0: Crouch double as Liverpool demolish Costa Ricans to reach showpiece final |work=The Independent |location=London |date=16 December 2005 |access-date=29 August 2011 |first=Andy |last=Hunter }}{{cbignore}}</ref>

==Match==
===Background===
[[File:Yokohamakokusai20041208.jpg|thumb|The [[International Stadium Yokohama|International Stadium]] in Yokohama was the venue for the final]]

Both teams were well into their respective domestic competitions. São Paulo's domestic season had finished two weeks before the final. They finished the [[2005 Campeonato Brasileiro Série A]] in 11th position after 42 games, they won and lost 16 games each and drew 10.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rsssfbrasil.com/tablesae/br2005.htm |title=Brazil 2005 Championship – First Level (Série A) |publisher=Rec. Sport. Soccer Statistics Foundation (RSSSF) |date=8 December 2005 |access-date=29 August 2011 |author=RSSSF Brazil }}</ref> Liverpool were in the middle of their domestic season, they were competing in the [[2005–06 FA Premier League|2005–06 Premier League]]. They were second in the table and won their last match before the competition 2–0, against [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]].<ref name="fifa">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=4735/news/newsid=102150.html#europe+south+america+battle |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071219182614/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=4735/news/newsid=102150.html#europe+south+america+battle |url-status=dead |archive-date=19 December 2007 |title=Europe and South America do battle |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association (FIFA) |date=17 December 2005 |access-date=29 August 2011 }}</ref> The victory meant Liverpool had gone 10 matches without conceding a goal, which equalled a club record.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/4494768.stm |title=Liverpool 2–0 Middlesbrough |work=BBC Sport |date=10 December 2005 |access-date=29 August 2011 }}</ref>

São Paulo manager [[Paulo Autuori]] was adamant that his team would have to improve on their performance in the semi-final if they were to win the match: "We're going to have to improve certain aspects of our play if we want to win the final." Autuori was not worried about Liverpool's aerial threat, but was expecting them to keep the ball during the match: "English teams are well known for their aerial excellence, But Liverpool are a side that can keep the ball very well too as they proved when winning the Champions League."<ref name="fifa" />

Liverpool manager [[Rafael Benítez]] would take control of the team for the final, despite the death of his father earlier in the week. Benitez was confident that his Liverpool team would be capable of beating São Paulo: "We are playing with confidence and strength. I think we can beat anyone." [[Peter Crouch]], who scored twice in the semi-finals, was as adamant as his manager about winning the competition. Adding that "We've not travelled all the way to Japan to go sightseeing."<ref name="fifa" />

===First half===
[[File:Rogério Ceni.jpg|thumb|left|alt=A man looking straight ahead with the top half of a suit visible|[[Rogério Ceni]] captained São Paulo in the final.]]
Before the start of the match, a minute's silence was held in memory of Rafa Benítez's father Francisco, who died of heart failure on the Wednesday before the final. Both teams took the pitch wearing black armbands as a mark of respect.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2005/12/18/sports/18iht-CUP.html?scp=6&sq=brazil%27s%20soul&st=Search |title=São Paulo survives to win world title |work=The New York Times |date=19 December 2005 |access-date=27 October 2011 |first=Rob |last=Hughes }}</ref> The match was delayed further when a fan of [[Sport Club Corinthians Paulista|Corinthians]] invaded the pitch and gave São Paulo goalkeeper [[Rogério Ceni]] a [[Bambi (character)|Bambi]] toy, referencing the ''Bambis'' nickname that city rivals associate São Paulo with.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/photo_galleries/4539478.stm |title=Liverpool v Sao Paulo photos |work=BBC Sport |date=18 December 2005 |access-date=27 October 2011 }}</ref> São Paulo lined up in a 3–5–2 formation, while Liverpool opted for a 4–4–1–1.<ref name="guardian">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2005/dec/19/newsstory.sport1 |title=Benítez makes beeline for Blatter after night of frustration |work=The Guardian |location=London |date=19 December 2005 |access-date=30 August 2011 |first=Dominic |last=Fifield }}</ref> Liverpool had the first chance of the match inside the opening 79 seconds, when captain [[Steven Gerrard]]'s cross into the penalty area was met by [[Fernando Morientes]], but the Spaniard headed wide. The Reds created more chances with [[Luis García (footballer, born 1978)|Luis García]] missing twice from headers.<ref name="cnn">{{cite news|url=http://edition.cnn.com/2005/SPORT/football/12/18/soccer.japan/ |title=Sao Paulo lifts World Clubs trophy |publisher=CNN |date=20 December 2005 |access-date=30 August 2011 }}</ref> São Paulo had only created one chance, a shot from striker [[Márcio Amoroso|Amoroso]], but they began to grow in confidence. They pressed forward after Amoroso's shot and right-back [[Cicinho]] nearly scored with a lob from the halfway line which just went over the crossbar.<ref name="report">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=4735/news/newsid=102060.html#sao+paulo+strike+gold+south+america |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071105144558/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=4735/news/newsid=102060.html#sao+paulo+strike+gold+south+america |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 November 2007 |title=Sao Paulo strike gold for South America |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association (FIFA) |date=18 December 2005 |access-date=30 August 2011 }}</ref>

São Paulo's pressure resulted in them taking the lead in the 27th minute, when a chipped pass from [[Aloísio Chulapa|Aloísio]] found [[Mineiro (footballer, born 1975)|Mineiro]], who put the ball beyond Liverpool goalkeeper [[Pepe Reina]] to give the Brazilians a 1–0 lead.<ref name="bbc">{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/4533980.stm |title=Sao Paulo 1-0 Liverpool |work=BBC Sport |date=18 December 2005 |access-date=30 August 2011 }}</ref> It was the first goal Liverpool had conceded in over 1,041 minutes of play.<ref name="guardian" /> Immediately afterwards, from a corner, [[Xabi Alonso]] almost assisted García if not for the woodwork.<ref name="bbc" /> Liverpool had another chance 30 minutes into the match, but again he failed to score with a header.<ref name="bbc" /> Gerrard had a chance to equalise five minutes later, but he put his shot wide after [[Harry Kewell]] had passed him the ball.<ref name="cnn" /> Liverpool continued to create chances and in the 39th minute, García saw his header into the far corner saved by Ceni.<ref name="report" /> From the resulting corner, Liverpool defender [[Sami Hyypiä]] shot straight at Ceni, who also saved a header from the Finn late in the first half.<ref name="report" />

===Second half===
Liverpool had the first scoring opportunity of the half, six minutes into the second half. Liverpool were awarded a free kick 25 yards from the goal. Gerrard's subsequent shot was saved by Ceni.<ref name="report" /> Ceni made another save soon afterwards when he tipped a cross from Kewell onto the crossbar.<ref name="report" /> Liverpool did score on the hour mark, but the goal was disallowed. García's headed goal was ruled out for offside.<ref name="bbc" /> Soon afterwards Morientes had another chance to score, but his overhead kick went over the crossbar.<ref name="cnn" /> Liverpool had another goal disallowed soon afterwards. Alonso's corner from which Hyypiä was adjudged to have drifted out of play before the defender scored.<ref name="guardian" />

São Paulo made the first substitution in the 75th minute when [[Grafite]] replaced Aloísio. Liverpool made two substitutions four minutes later in an attempt to equalise. [[John Arne Riise]] and [[Florent Sinama Pongolle|Sinama Pongolle]] replaced [[Stephen Warnock]] and [[Mohamed Sissoko]]. They made their final substitution in the 85th minute when Peter Crouch replaced Morientes.<ref name="stats">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=4735/matches/match=47350007/report.html |title=Match report |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association (FIFA) |date=18 December 2005 |access-date=30 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111020191613/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament%3D107/edition%3D4735/matches/match%3D47350007/report.html |archive-date=20 October 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Liverpool had a third goal disallowed in the 89th minute; substitute Sinama Pongolle's goal was disallowed after the assistant referee had flagged García as being offside in the build-up to the goal.<ref name="guardian" /> Liverpool had a final chance before the end of the match, but García put his shot wide. No further goals were scored and the referee blew for full-time with the final score 1–0 to São Paulo.<ref name="bbc" />

